#{[17592186531747 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531733 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531747 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531747 #edb/ref 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,-4,-6)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{3x}y - 3x^2y+3\\cos(\\pi y)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531747 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43957037-7bd8-5b6e-bc6b-591297b827c3" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531747 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(3,-4,-6)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,-4).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,-4) & =\\frac{3(3)}{(-4)}-3(3)^2(-4)+3\\cos(\\pi (-4)) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{9}{4})+(108)+(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{435}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531767 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531786 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531767 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531767 #edb/ref 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,3,6)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{5x}y - 5x^2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531767 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "766caef0-3a57-5700-b82a-867a65ef10cf" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531767 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,3,6)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,3) & =\\frac{5(2)}{(3)}-5(2)^2(3) \\\\  & =(\\frac{10}{3})+(-60) \\\\  & =- \\frac{170}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531778 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531748 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531778 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531778 #edb/ref 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,3,7)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{2x}y - 2x^2y+3\\cos(\\pi y)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531778 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97d3413e-8179-53f9-a06b-822f3537361d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531778 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,3,7)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,3) & =\\frac{2(2)}{(3)}-2(2)^2(3)+3\\cos(\\pi (3)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3})+(-24)+(-3) \\\\  & =- \\frac{77}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531780 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531787 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531780 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531780 #edb/ref 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,3,-4)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{5x}y - 5x^2y+3\\cos(\\pi y)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531780 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "990f5ea4-fca7-553b-a806-3011750520cf" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531780 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,3,-4)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,3) & =\\frac{5(2)}{(3)}-5(2)^2(3)+3\\cos(\\pi (3)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{10}{3})+(-60)+(-3) \\\\  & =- \\frac{179}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531781 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531791 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531781 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531781 #edb/ref 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,-4,0)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{3x}y - 3x^2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531781 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9bb0f096-d343-50e6-ab19-61c16c1aaf3c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531781 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(3,-4,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,-4).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,-4) & =\\frac{3(3)}{(-4)}-3(3)^2(-4) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{9}{4})+(108) \\\\  & =\\frac{423}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531792 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531775 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531792 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531792 #edb/ref 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,-3,-3)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531792 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c56af156-4a29-5a55-8ac3-c69f2feb8c74" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531792 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(-2,-3,-3)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,-3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,-3) & =\\frac{4(-2)}{(-3)}-4(-2)^2(-3) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3})+(48) \\\\  & =\\frac{152}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531794 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531754 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531794 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531794 #edb/ref 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,4,5)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{5x}y - 5x^2y+4\\cos(\\pi y)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531794 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4415dff-6625-5061-8a7a-46e392547880" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531794 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(3,4,5)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,4).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,4) & =\\frac{5(3)}{(4)}-5(3)^2(4)+4\\cos(\\pi (4)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{15}{4})+(-180)+(4) \\\\  & =- \\frac{689}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531800 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531766 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531800 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531800 #edb/ref 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=-3.$ Given that the point $P(-3,4,4)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{2x}y - 2x^2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531800 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb25f684-725c-58f3-8f3d-89177734553d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531800 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(-3,4,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-3,4).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-3,4) & =\\frac{2(-3)}{(4)}-2(-3)^2(4) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{3}{2})+(-72) \\\\  & =- \\frac{147}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531801 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186124437.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186531801 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "06977e38-001b-5371-84fb-705d2090e87e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556105 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186556134 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556105 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556105 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(-2)x$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556105 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "055263bb-00e6-5ecd-9052-3f0991382dda" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556105 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,2) & =(-2)(-2)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{x}(-2,2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =(4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =4 x + 8 \\\\ z & =4 x + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=2 \\\\ z=4 x + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=4 x + 8$ (or $4 x - z + 8=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨4,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨4,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =- i - 4 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-4⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,-4⟩)=⟨1,0,4⟩$ is also parallel to it." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556111 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186556137 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556111 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556111 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=4.$ Given that the point $P(-2,4,-4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556111 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24dbd77f-2590-58ef-821c-6bd1e8a62bdc" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556111 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,4).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,4) & ={f}_{x}(-2,4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =(3)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =3 x + 6 \\\\ z & =3 x + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,4,-4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=4 \\\\ z=3 x + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=4,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3 x + 2$ (or $3 x - z + 2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =- i - 3 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,-3⟩)=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186556129 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556113 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556113 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,3,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=(-2)y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "322047e2-3976-5636-bcd1-e92ee6b570c4" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556113 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,3) & =(-2)(3)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(3,3) & ={f}_{y}(3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(-6)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =18 - 6 y \\\\ z & =15 - 6 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,3,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=3 \\\\ z=15 - 6 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=15 - 6 y$ (or $- 6 y - z + 15=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-6,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-6,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j - 6 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,-6⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556118 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186556133 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556118 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556118 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,3,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556118 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48d0933d-38f4-5fca-ac17-2cb0bd7b696b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556118 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,3) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(3,3) & ={f}_{y}(3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(-2)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =6 - 2 y \\\\ z & =3 - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,3,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=3 \\\\ z=3 - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3 - 2 y$ (or $- 2 y - z + 3=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j - 2 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,-2⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556132 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186556148 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556132 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556132 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=8$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556132 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "76fc75e5-bc34-5a11-827a-718027bfa6d6" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186556132 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,2) & ={f}_{y}(1,2)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(8)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =8 y - 16 \\\\ z & =8 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=1 \\\\ z=8 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=8 y - 19$ (or $8 y - z - 19=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=